repl_edit — implementation plan
================================

status: planned (2026-07-09)

references I1..I5 (plans/repl_list). pass-through ("edit" => keys
["repl", "idx", "isar_text"]). semantics to pin: edit re-executes the
edited step immediately; later steps go stale (or replay right away
when auto_replay is on — engine default matters to the description).

advertised metadata (the builtin table row)
-------------------------------------------

name          repl_edit
description   "Replace the step at index `idx` with new Isar text and
              re-execute it from that point's pre-state. Subsequent
              steps are marked stale; run repl_replay to re-execute
              them (or they replay automatically if auto-replay is
              enabled). If the new text FAILS, the REPL is unchanged
              — the old step survives. repl_edit edits the REPL's
              step list, NOT the theory file on disk."
              (source: mcp_server.py edit :339, plus the failure
              atomicity of with_claim/edit_repl and the spec's "repl
              edits are not file edits" warning.)
inputSchema   {"type": "object",
               "properties": {
                 "repl": {"type": "string"},
                 "idx": {"type": "integer"},
                 "isar_text": {"type": "string"}},
               "required": ["repl", "idx", "isar_text"]}
annotations   {"readOnlyHint": false, "idempotentHint": false,
               "openWorldHint": false}
outputSchema  none.

name/argument mapping across the layers
---------------------------------------

  mcp client edge   tools/call "repl_edit",
                    {"repl":"T","idx":0,"isar_text":"by auto"}
  scala builtin row backend.ir("edit", [(repl,T),(idx,"0"),
                    (isar_text,"by auto")])
  ml dispatcher     "edit" -> Ir.edit (get "repl") (get_int "idx")
                    (get "isar_text")
  engine            ir.ML:628 — with_claim Op_Edit; edit_repl:610
                    re-executes at idx, marks the tail stale, replays
                    it if #auto_replay cfg; out "Edited step N, M
                    subsequent steps marked stale"

implementation steps
--------------------

1. [decide, description-level] what auto_replay defaults to in OUR
   server (ir.ML cfg default; mcp_server.py exposed set_auto_replay,
   we do not plan to). read the cfg record default while implementing
   and make the description state the actual behavior instead of the
   conditional sentence above.
2. [this tool] builtin row + handler; errors -> isError.
3. tests; rebuild per rules.

assumptions and how to test them
--------------------------------

T1  idx is a plain 0-based step index; negatives are NOT supported
    here (unlike truncate/fork/state) — out of range error.
    test [ml unit]: edit at -1 -> error "out of range"; edit at n ->
    error; edit at 0 -> ok.
T2  failure atomicity: a failing edit leaves the old step and no
    staleness behind, claim released.
    test [ml unit]: 2 steps; edit 0 with garbage -> error; show ->
    original text, step 1 not stale; repl still steps.
T3  staleness bookkeeping matches the reply text: editing step i of n
    marks exactly n-i-1 steps stale (auto_replay off), and
    repl_replay clears them.
    test [ml unit]: 3 steps, edit 0 -> "2 subsequent steps marked
    stale"; show -> [stale] on steps 1,2; replay -> "Replayed 2";
    show -> clean.
T4  pin interaction: editing a pinned repl marks the pin stale
    (mark_pin_stale on mutation), so dependents rebase honestly.
    test [ml unit]: pin, edit, show -> "pinned [stale]".
    (covered jointly with plans/repl_pin.)
T5  full chain.
    test [e2e]: step a failing 'by simp', repl_edit it to 'by auto',
    repl_text shows the fixed script.

done when
---------

- T1..T5 green; description states the real auto_replay default;
  tools/list shows the row above.

status: done (2026-07-09). T1..T5 green at all layers. Step 1's decision:
this server's engine default is auto_replay=true (ir.ML:65) and there is
no MCP.ir fname to change it, so the description states that plainly
("auto-replay is always on in this server ... no separate repl_replay
call is needed") instead of the plan's conditional phrasing. ml unit
(mcp/Tools/HOL/Tests/MCP_Repl_Tests.thy): T1 idx bounds (negative and
past-end both "out of range", 0 valid), T2 failure atomicity (bad tactic
leaves the old step and no staleness, claim released, repl still
steps), T3 the reply's stale count is accurate but -- per the auto_replay
finding -- show finds them already clean by the time it's called, not
stale (same class of gap as plans/repl_show T1, documented inline rather
than silently adjusted), T4 editing a pinned repl marks the pin stale
regardless of operation kind (release always calls mark_pin_stale).
scala unit + tools/call wiring in isabelle mcp_test; no new bridge test
needed (T1-T4 have no async dimension of their own, distinct from
repl_step/repl_show/repl_remove). e2e (test_mcp.py's test_repl_builtins):
step a lemma and its proof, repl_edit the proof step's tactic, confirm
repl_show/repl_text reflect the edit -- the plan's literal
"edit a failing step" framing does not fit the API (a failing repl_step
is never added, so there is no index to edit), so T5 exercises
edit-a-successful-step instead; the failure-atomicity promise itself is
already covered at the ml-unit layer (T2).
